# a-list-that-is-mixed — plan 31 follow-up, and what the composition costs.
#
# ⚠⚠ **The same base as `a-list-of-one-symbol.keys` with the `schedule`
# line REMOVED**, so it plays the schedule every `WaveState` is born
# with — which since 2026-08-29 carries the design's composition from
# `examples/waves.json` § parts — which is the ONLY place the
# composition lives, `waves.loft` holding no copy of it.
#
# ⚠ So the pair is one line apart and the line is *author a flat list*
# against *take the shipped one*.  Same ground, wall, tower, crew,
# spawn, poke and ticks.
#
# ⚠⚠ **The SIZES are identical** — 5 / 8 / 12 / 20 / 30 / 50 / 80 in
# both — so what moves the clock is the MIX and can be nothing else.
# That is `@M020`'s reading applied on purpose: a wave is worth its
# front class PLUS what the front cannot cover, so a composition at
# constant size is the change worth measuring.
#
# ⚠ Waves 0 and 1 carry no part in either file: the opening five and
# eight are the player's first sight of a robot and `@X246`'s neutral
# landing zone wants them legible.  The mix starts at wave 2.

echo a list that is mixed: the same counts, composed

do toggle_mode
do cycle_kind
click 0 0
marker 0 0 target
do cycle_kind
do cycle_kind
do cycle_kind
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
click 20 0
marker 20 0 spawn 3
count markers 2 2
do toggle_mode

palette 5
drag -4 -3 22 -3
drag -4 -2 22 -2
drag -4 -1 22 -1
drag -4 0 22 0
drag -4 1 22 1
drag -4 2 22 2
drag -4 3 22 3

# A wall to chew, and a tower behind it — the difference between the
# classes is a WALL-DAMAGE rate and a SPEED, so a base with nothing to
# chew could not tell them apart at all.
# ⚠ A GATE at (6, 0) — the wall is painted around it — so the player can
# drive out to poke the marker and back.  `plans/12` B7 measured that a
# wall with a gate in it buys ONE tick over a sealed one, and it is
# identical in both halves of this pair either way.
palette 9
click 6 -3
click 6 -2
click 6 -1
click 6 1
click 6 2
click 6 3

# ⚠⚠ **Three towers and two crew, and the base needs every one of
# them**: the design's mix starts at WAVE 2, so a base that cannot reach
# wave 2 cannot tell the two halves of this pair apart at all.  The
# first version of this fixture had one tower, fell at 175 having sent
# two waves, and read **175 against 175** — a flat pair measuring a base
# that died before the content it was measuring arrived.
do toggle_mode
do cycle_kind
do cycle_kind
click 3 -2
marker 3 -2 tower
click 1 0
marker 1 0 tower
click 3 2
marker 3 2 tower
do toggle_mode
top 3 -2 on
top 1 0 on
top 3 2 on

# ⚠ The crew repair the towers between waves — `plans/17` T1 measured
# that without upkeep more towers buy NOTHING (3, 5 and 7 towers give
# 321, 319 and 317 ticks and four waves each).
crew 2 -1
crew 2 1
park 0 0

# ⚠⚠ **No `schedule` line — that is the whole difference.**  The state
# is born with the design's list already composed.

# ── The poke ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ⚠ The wave list does not start until the player drives out and touches
# a spawn marker 12+ hexes from the core (`WAVE_1_PROVOCATION_HEXES`),
# so both files start their clock the same way and at the same tick.
waves 0 0
drive 20 0
tick 14
vehicle 20 0
waves 1 1

drive 0 0
tick 14
vehicle 0 0


# ── ⚠⚠ THE READING: 452 AGAINST 452 — THE MIX COSTS NOTHING ────
#
# **Same base, same counts, same poke — and the same clock to the tick.**
# The composed list is exactly as hard as the flat one, which is what
# the owner asked for and is NOT what a mix does by default.
#
# ⚠⚠ **THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS FILE READ 515 AGAINST 452 — a mix
# SOFTENED the list by 14 %** — and the retune is the finding (`@M090`).
# Swept over pure compositions on this base:
#
#     all regular      452        all miner        752
#     all builder      452        all harvester    never falls
#     all scout        671
#
# ⚠⚠ **The siege front is the wall's WIDTH (`@M020`), so a fixed number
# of robots chew at once and the rest queue — which means a wave is
# decided by the WORST class holding a slot.**  A scout moves 2.5 hex/s
# against a regular's 1.5 and does **0.1 wall damage against 1.0**, so
# scouts arrive first, take every slot and chew at a tenth of the rate
# while the triple-damage miners are still walking.
#
# ⚠⚠ **AND MORE BODIES MAKE IT WORSE, WHICH IS THE COUNTER-INTUITIVE
# HALF**: the authored counts PLUS extra weak bodies read 641 (scouts),
# 534 (miners) and 660 (both) against the flat 452.  A wave cannot be
# strengthened by adding to it — only diluted.
#
# ⚠ **So `builder` is the one class that can join a wave for free**:
# 1.5 hex/s like a regular, and its 1.5x wall damage buys nothing
# because the breach clock is already saturated — all-builder ties
# all-regular to the tick.  ⚠⚠ **A richer mix is not a tuning problem
# but a RATES problem**, and rates are balance — deferred to the economy
# (`ROADMAP.md` item 10, § THE SESSION IS THE GAP NOW).
#
# ⚠ And the tuning was NOT fitted to this number: one scout per wave
# read 620 and two read 453, which is non-monotonic — evidence that
# fitting a composition to a tick count is fitting noise.  What is
# shipped is the structural claim, not the fitted one.
fall 900
ticks 448 456
